  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Oven temperatures</h2>
    <p>Recipes rarely use the exact conversion. Ovens marked in Celsius usually step in 10° or 20° increments, so the common pairs are rounded:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Recipe says</th><th class="num">Exact Celsius</th><th class="num">Set a Celsius oven to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>325°F</td><td class="num">162.8°C</td><td class="num">160 to 170°C</td></tr>
      <tr><td>350°F</td><td class="num">176.7°C</td><td class="num">180°C</td></tr>
      <tr><td>375°F</td><td class="num">190.6°C</td><td class="num">190°C</td></tr>
      <tr><td>400°F</td><td class="num">204.4°C</td><td class="num">200°C</td></tr>
      <tr><td>425°F</td><td class="num">218.3°C</td><td class="num">220°C</td></tr>
      <tr><td>450°F</td><td class="num">232.2°C</td><td class="num">230°C</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Recipes written for fan (convection) ovens are commonly set about 20°C or 25°F lower than for conventional ovens, because moving air cooks faster. Check which type a recipe assumes before converting.</p>

    <h2>Converting in your head</h2>
    <p>For weather, double the Celsius figure and add 30. It is exact at 10°C (50°F) and stays within 2°F between 0°C and 20°C, but it drifts as temperatures climb: at 30°C it gives 90°F against an exact 86°F. Going the other way, subtract 30 from Fahrenheit and halve it. For cooking or anything medical, use the converter.</p>

    <h2>Readings vs. differences</h2>
    <p>The formula F = C × 9/5 + 32 converts a reading. A change in temperature converts without the 32: a rise of 10°C is a rise of 18°F, not 50°F. This matters when converting a forecast's "5 degrees warmer than yesterday," a thermostat setback, or a lab tolerance of ±2°C (which is ±3.6°F).</p>

    <h2>Kelvin and altitude</h2>
    <p>Kelvin starts at absolute zero, so it has no negative values and is written without a degree sign: 20°C is 293.15 K. It is used whenever a formula needs an absolute temperature, such as gas laws.</p>
    <p>Water's boiling point drops with altitude, by roughly 1°F for every 500 ft. In Denver, about 5,280 ft up, water boils near 202°F (about 95°C), which is why high-altitude recipes add cooking time or adjust ingredients.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Temperature conversion questions</h2>
    <details><summary>What is 350°F in Celsius?</summary><p>350°F is 176.7°C. Ovens marked in Celsius are usually set to 180°C for recipes that call for 350°F.</p></details>
    <details><summary>At what temperature are Fahrenheit and Celsius equal?</summary><p>At −40. Minus 40°F is exactly minus 40°C.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is normal body temperature in Celsius?</summary><p>The traditional figure of 98.6°F is 37°C. Normal readings vary from person to person and over the day.</p></details>
  </section>
